[candidate · 3:27] Yes, let me show the math. Say revenue two years ago is 100. Profit is 24 percent, so 24.

Now revenue is 115, because 15 percent growth. Margin 6 percent, so profit is 115 times 0.06 — that is 6.9.

So 6.9 divided by 24 is... about 0.29. So yes, roughly 29 percent, so slightly less than one-third of before. Profit fall around 70 percent.

And — this is useful, because same 200 stores. So the growth is not from new stores. It must come from price, or from volume per store, or mix — maybe they sell more food, more expensive drinks.

So if revenue per store go up 15 percent but profit collapse, the cost is growing much faster than revenue. In absolute: cost before is 76, now cost is 115 minus 6.9, so 108.1. That is 42 percent increase in cost, against 15 percent in revenue. So I think the problem is mostly cost side.
