[interviewer · CLARIFY · 3:30] Okay. Your framework has revenue and cost, with fixed and variable inside cost. Is that MECE, and what's missing?

[candidate silent 15s]

[candidate · 5:15] Hmm, let me think. Revenue and cost is MECE, yes, that part is fine — profit is revenue minus cost, nothing else.

But inside cost, I think I was... too narrow. Two things missing. First, I say net profit margin, but net is after interest, tax, depreciation. So maybe cost of operations did not move so much, and instead they take debt, interest expense up, or a one-time write-off. So I should separate operating cost from non-operating — interest, tax, impairment.

Second — sorry, how to say — my fixed and variable split is by behavior, but it is maybe more useful to split by function: cost of goods sold, store-level operating cost like labor and rent and utility, then corporate overhead. Because the client can act on these differently.

Also on revenue I only said price and volume, but I should add mix, because mix change can move the margin without moving revenue much.

Can we see the cost breakdown as percent of revenue, two years ago versus today?
