[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 7:13] Okay. Menu prices have not changed in two years. Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit. Average revenue per store is $2.4M a year.

[candidate silent 20s]

[candidate · 9:03] Let me check if it ties. Beans 25 percent of 42 is 10.5 points of revenue. Up 40 percent, that is 14.7. The other 75 percent is 31.5 points, up 37.5 percent, that is 31.5 times 1.375 — about 43.3. Sum is 58.0. That matches exactly the current COGS.

So the whole COGS increase is explained by input inflation, blended around 38 percent. Nothing suspicious like waste or theft or bad procurement — just prices of input.

And the second half: menu price flat, zero percent, in two years. So they absorbed 38 percent input inflation with zero price action. The revenue grew 15 percent only because the ticket grew 10 percent from more items — which actually makes it worse, because more items means more COGS too, no price margin.

So root cause: pricing has not followed cost. Can I size what price increase would be needed to restore margin?
