[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 9:06] Yes, go ahead and size it. Walk me through your approach, and state any assumptions you make.

[candidate · 10:36] Assumptions: volume stays same, labor and overhead stay same in dollars, COGS in dollars does not change because units are same. Base 100 of revenue.

Today: COGS 58, labor 22, overhead 14 — total cost 94, profit 6.

If I raise price by x, revenue is 100 times (1+x), cost stays 94. For 24 percent margin:
100(1+x) − 94 = 24(1+x)
76(1+x) = 94
1+x = 1.237.

So about 24 percent price increase. On ticket, 6.80 times 1.237 is about 8.41. That is big — I think too big to do at one time.

More realistic target, say back to 15 percent margin: 85(1+x) = 94, so 1+x = 1.106, about 10.6 percent. Ticket goes 6.80 to about 7.52, roughly 70 cents.

Risk is elasticity. Breakeven on volume: at plus 10.6 percent price, contribution per unit goes from 0.42 to 0.526 of old revenue — so I can lose about 20 percent of volume before I am worse off. That is a decent cushion.
