[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 10:39] Understood. Beyond pricing, what else could the client do to restore margin?

[candidate silent 20s]

[candidate · 12:29] Few other levers, I group in three.

First, cost of input itself. They buy beans at spot, apparently — so they take full 40 percent. They could do forward contracts or hedging, lock price for twelve, eighteen months. Also direct sourcing from farms, or consolidate suppliers for volume discount. At 200 stores times 2.4 million, that is 480 million revenue, so 278 million of COGS — even 5 percent saving is almost 14 million.

Second, mix and portion. Not all items inflate same. Dairy is up a lot — so push drinks with less dairy, or smaller milk portion, cup size engineering. And push high-margin items, food or pastry, if margin there is better. Honestly I would like to see margin by item, we don't have it.

Third — the ticket already grew 10 percent on items per visit, so the customer is willing. So bundling, upsell, loyalty program to protect volume when price goes up.

Labor flat at 22 percent I would not touch — it is already controlled, and cutting it hurts service exactly when price is rising.
