[interviewer · WRAP · 12:32] Walk me through the figure you gave for COGS in dollars. You said they buy beans at spot, which is an assumption. How do you know that, and does the data support it?

[candidate · 14:02] The dollar figure: 200 stores times 2.4 million per store is 480 million revenue. COGS is 58 percent of that, so 0.58 times 480 — 0.58 times 48 is 27.8 — so 278 million. Yes, that one I am comfortable with.

The spot purchasing, you are right, I should not have said it like a fact. I inferred it, but the inference is weak. What the data actually shows is only that their realized input cost rose 40 percent, roughly in line with the market move. That is consistent with spot buying, but it is also consistent with a contract that expired, or an annual repricing with the supplier, or a hedge that covered only part of the volume.

So the correct statement is: they have no effective protection against input price moves. Whether the mechanism is spot or a weak contract, I don't know. To test it I would ask for procurement contracts — length, volume committed, repricing terms — and compare their realized bean cost per pound against the market index.
