[interviewer · WRAP · 15:37] Understood. What's your recommendation to the CEO?

[candidate silent 15s]

[candidate · 17:22] My recommendation: Brew & Bean should raise menu prices, phased, roughly 10 to 12 percent over twelve months, and at the same time fix procurement. That is the answer.

Why. The decline is almost entirely one line — COGS went 42 to 58 percent of revenue, and that is 16 of the 18 points lost. Labor is flat at 22, overhead moved only two points. And the COGS increase is fully explained by input inflation, blended 38 percent, while menu price moved zero. So this is not an operations problem, it is a pricing problem.

Size: about 70 million of annual profit lost. A 10.6 percent price rise is about 51 million, and 5 percent procurement saving is about 14 million.

Risks. Volume — but breakeven is roughly 20 percent volume loss, and ticket already grew 10 percent on items, so demand looks healthy. Also competitor response, and brand perception in a specialty segment.

Next steps: pilot the price rise in twenty stores for eight weeks to measure real elasticity, audit the bean contracts, and get item-level margin so we price selectively, not flat across the menu.
